"Spherical Steering: Geometry-Aware Activation Rotation for Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08169v1)\nciteturn31641view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view0\",\"lineno\":225}); Total lines: 533\nL191:  | $\\displaystyle\\displaystyle p_{T}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{e^{\\kappa s_{T}}}{e^{\\kappa s_{T}}+e^{\\kappa s_{H}}},$  |  | (13)\nL192:  | $\\displaystyle\\displaystyle p_{H}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{e^{\\kappa s_{H}}}{e^{\\kappa s_{T}}+e^{\\kappa s_{H}}}.$  |\nL193: \nL194: We summarize the tendency toward the hallucinated direction by:\nL195: \nL196:  | $$\\delta=p_{H}-p_{T}\\in[-1,1].$$  |  | (14)\nL197: \nL198: We then map this confidence score to an intervention strength $t\\in[0,1]$ via a thresholded, bounded rule:\nL199:  | $$t=\\begin{cases}0,&\\delta\\leq\\beta,\\\\[8.0pt]\nL200: \\displaystyle\\mathrm{clip}\\!\\left(\\alpha\\cdot\\frac{\\delta-\\beta}{1-\\beta},\\,0,\\,1\\right),&\\delta>\\beta,\\end{cases}$$  |  | (15)\nL201: where $\\beta\\in[-1,1)$ controls steering conservativeness, $\\alpha\\in(0,1]$ scales the rotation (and thus the maximum adjustment), and $\\kappa$ controls how sharply the confidence changes with alignment (as induced by the vMF concentration parameter). In summary, the gate computes $t$ from $\\hat{h}^{(l)}$, yielding $t=0$ when steering is unnecessary and larger $t$ when the activation is more aligned with the hallucinated direction.\nL202: ### 3.4 Inference-Time Procedure\nL203: \nL204: We now describe the complete inference procedure that integrates the components from §cite11†3.1 –§cite17†3.3 .\nL205: ##### Generation Procedure.\nL206: \nL207: Given an input prompt and the selected steering layers $\\mathcal{L}=\\{l_{1},\\ldots,l_{K}\\}$ with their corresponding prototypes $\\{\\mu_{T}^{(l)}\\}_{l\\in\\mathcal{L}}$, we generate tokens autoregressively. At each decoding step $j$, for each layer $l\\in\\mathcal{L}$:\nL208: \nL209:   * •\nL210: \nL211: Extract the current token’s activation $h^{(l)}_{j}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ and normalize: $\\hat{h}^{(l)}_{j}=h^{(l)}_{j}/\\|h^{(l)}_{j}\\|$.\nL212: \nL213:   * •\nL214: Compute alignment scores: $s_{T}=\\mu_{T}^{\\top}\\hat{h}^{(l)}_{j}$ and $s_{H}=\\mu_{H}^{\\top}\\hat{h}^{(l)}_{j}$ respectively.\nL215: \nL216:   * •\nL217: \nL218: Apply vMF gate (Eq. cite116†15 ) to obtain steering strength $t_{j}$.\nL219: \nL220:   * •\nL221: \nL222: If $t_{j}>0$, rotate via Slerp and restore magnitude: $h^{(l)\\prime}_{j}=\\|h^{(l)}_{j}\\|\\cdot\\text{Slerp}(\\hat{h}^{(l)}_{j},\\mu_{T},t_{j})$; otherwise keep $h^{(l)\\prime}_{j}=h^{(l)}_{j}$.\nL223: The intervened activation $h^{(l)\\prime}_{j}$ is then passed to subsequent layers. The confidence gate enables input-adaptive steering where $t_{j}$ varies across tokens based on their directional alignment, automatically reducing intervention when the model is already confident and truthful.\nL224: ## 4 Experiments\nL225: We present a comprehensive empirical evaluation of Spherical Steering across diverse reasoning benchmarks.\nL226: Our experiments reveal three key findings: (1) Spherical Steering achieves state-of-the-art multiple-choice performance, surpassing activation addition baselines by substantial margins; (2) unlike additive methods that degrade generation quality, our approach simultaneously improves both accuracy and fluency; and (3) norm-preserving rotation yields superior collapse-efficiency, achieving stronger performance gains with less representational degradation.\nL227: ### 4.1 Experimental Setup\nL228: ##### Benchmarks.\nL229: We evaluate on two complementary task categories: (1) Multiple-choice reasoning, where models select the most plausible answer from a fixed set of candidates using likelihood scoring. We assess performance on TruthfulQA (MC1/MC2/MC3) (cite117†Lin et al., 2022 ), COPA (cite118†Gordon et al., 2012 ), StoryCloze (cite119†Mostafazadeh et al., 2016 ), BoolQ (cite120†Clark et al., 2019 ), MMLU (cite121†Hendrycks et al., 2020 ), and WinoGrande (cite122†Sakaguchi et al., 2020 ).\nL230: (2) Open-ended generation, where models produce free-form answers. We evaluate using TruthfulQA’s generative setting with two pretrained judge models measuring truthfulness (TRUE) and informativeness (INFO), with TRUE$\\times$INFO serving as the primary metric (cite79†Li et al., 2023 ). Please refer to Appendix cite46†C.2 for detailed experimental setups.\nL231: ##### Models.\nL232: \nL233: We evaluate on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct (cite91†Yang et al., 2024 ) and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (cite60†Dubey et al., 2024 ), two state-of-the-art open-source instruction-tuned models with distinct architectural characteristics.\nL234: ##### Baselines.\nL235: We compare against representative activation steering methods: (1) Inference-Time Intervention (ITI) (cite79†Li et al., 2023 ), which uses linear probes to identify steering heads; (2) Contrastive Activation Addition (CAA) (cite80†Rimsky et al., 2024 ), which applies layer-wise additive interventions; (3) SADI-HEAD (cite102†Wang et al., 2024 ), which uses dynamic element-wise scaling at attention heads; and (4) 5-shot In-Context Learning (ICL) as a prompt-only baseline.\nL236: All methods use identical data splits and undergo dedicated hyperparameter sweeps for fair comparison.\nL237: Table 1: TruthfulQA results on LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct. Metrics are in percentage (%). Key insight: Spherical Steering achieves the best multiple-choice performance while simultaneously maintaining or improving generation quality—a Pareto improvement over all baselines.\nL238: Model  | Method  | Multiple-Choice  | Open-ended Generation\nL239: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL240:  | MC1  | MC2  | MC3  | Avg.  | TRUE  | INFO  | TRUE$\\times$INFO\nL241: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL242: LLaMA- 3.1-8B- Instruct  | Baseline  | 34.15  | 53.32  | 27.02  | 38.16  | 82.32  | 58.60  | 48.24\nL243:  | 5-shot ICL  | 38.31  | 57.85  | 30.09  | 42.08  | 89.17  | 43.66  | 38.93\nL244:  | ITI (cite79†Li et al., 2023 )  | 37.70  | 58.09  | 30.12  | 41.97  | 89.66  | 44.96  | 40.31\nL245:  | CAA (cite80†Rimsky et al., 2024 )  | 35.99  | 56.26  | 29.36  | 40.54  | 84.63  | 58.67  | 49.66\nL246:  | SADI-HEAD (cite102†Wang et al., 2024 )  | 38.53  | 56.03  | 30.57  | 41.71  | 80.30  | 63.74  | 51.18\nL247:  | Spherical Steering (Ours)  | 49.95  | 68.51  | 41.05  | 53.17  | 82.05  | 66.57  | 54.63\nL248: Qwen- 2.5-7B- Instruct  | Baseline  | 35.87  | 54.95  | 26.62  | 39.15  | 83.65  | 88.93  | 74.40\nL249:  | 5-shot ICL  | 43.33  | 63.36  | 32.15  | 46.28  | 78.39  | 97.99  | 76.94\nL250:  | ITI (cite79†Li et al., 2023 )  | 40.15  | 58.93  | 30.26  | 43.11  | 99.60  | 68.10  | 67.82\nL251:  | CAA (cite80†Rimsky et al., 2024 )  | 40.16  | 59.08  | 30.41  | 43.22  | 98.99  | 68.63  | 67.95\nL252:  | SADI-HEAD (cite102†Wang et al., 2024 )  | 39.28  | 56.61  | 29.32  | 41.74  | 97.00  | 71.23  | 69.09\nL253:  | Spherical Steering (Ours)  | 48.71  | 66.90  | 39.16  | 51.59  | 88.02  | 88.44  | 77.84\nL254: ### 4.2 Main Results: Pareto-Optimal Accuracy Generation Trade-off\nL255: Table cite93†1 presents our primary results on TruthfulQA. Spherical Steering dominates all baselines in multiple-choice performance across both model families, achieving MC average improvements of +11.09% on LLaMA and +5.31% on Qwen over the best baseline. Remarkably, these accuracy gains do not come at the expense of generation quality—our method achieves the highest TRUE$\\times$INFO scores (+3.45% and +0.90% respectively), demonstrating a Pareto improvement over activation addition methods.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPretraining with Token-Level Adaptive Latent Chain-of-Thought (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08220v1)\nciteturn31641view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view1\",\"lineno\":145}); Total lines: 343\nL121: cite80†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Motivation for adaptive computation and the correctness-aware adaptive loss. We probe a latent-CoT model without adaptive computation on 0.34B tokens. At each latent step $k$, we bucket tokens by their current target-token probability $p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k)}$ (x-axis), and measure the average improvement in the target probability brought by the next latent step, $p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k+1)}-p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k)}$ (y-axis).\nL122: While extra latent computation substantially improves low-confidence tokens, the gains diminish as $p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k)}$ increases, and can become negative for already confident tokens (negative bars), suggesting unnecessary or even harmful computation.\nL123: ##### Router (conditional continuation).\nL124: \nL125: At each latent step $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,K_{\\max}\\}$, the Router predicts the conditional probability of continuing to the next latent step:\nL126: \nL127:  | $$g_{t}^{(k)}\\triangleq P(\\text{Continue}\\mid\\text{Reach step }k)=\\sigma\\!\\bigl(\\mathrm{Linear}(z_{t}^{(k)})\\bigr).$$  |  | (3)\nL128: \nL129: Accordingly, $P(\\text{Stop}\\mid\\text{Reach step }k)=1-g_{t}^{(k)}$.\nL130: ##### Reach probability (accumulated probability flow).\nL131: \nL132: For computation to exist at step $k$, it must have continued through all previous steps. We define the reach probability recursively:\nL133: \nL134:  | $$p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(1)}\\triangleq 1,\\qquad p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k+1)}\\triangleq p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k)}\\,g_{t}^{(k)}.$$  |  | (4)\nL135: ##### Exit probability (halting distribution).\nL136: \nL137: The probability of halting exactly at step $k$ is the joint probability of reaching step $k$ and deciding to stop:\nL138: \nL139:  | $$p_{\\mathrm{exit},t}^{(k)}\\triangleq P(K_{t}=k)=p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k)}\\bigl(1-g_{t}^{(k)}\\bigr).$$  |  | (5)\nL140: \nL141: We set $g_{t}^{(K_{\\max})}=0$ so that the process always halts by $K_{\\max}$, yielding $p_{\\mathrm{exit},t}^{(K_{\\max})}=p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(K_{\\max})}$.\nL142: ##### Truncation (threshold pruning).\nL143: \nL144: To save FLOPs, we avoid executing latent steps that are unlikely to be reached. After computing $g_{t}^{(k)}$, we estimate the probability of reaching the next step via $p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k+1)}$. If $p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k+1)}<\\tau$, we prune token $t$ from the batch for all subsequent steps. Equivalently, we define the last executed step:\nL145: \nL146:  | $$K_{t}^{\\star}\\triangleq\\max\\Bigl\\{k\\leq K_{\\max}\\ \\bigm|\\ p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k)}\\geq\\tau\\Bigr\\}.$$  |  | (6)\nL147: ##### Expectation-based mixing with residual re-allocation.\nL148: \nL149: We compute the final token representation as the expectation of latent-step states under the (truncated) halting distribution. Since pruning truncates the unrolled process, naïvely using $\\{p_{\\mathrm{exit},t}^{(k)}\\}_{k\\leq K_{t}^{\\star}}$ would discard probability mass beyond $K_{t}^{\\star}$. We therefore re-allocate the residual mass to the terminal executed state:\nL150:  | $$\\hat{p}_{\\mathrm{exit},t}^{(k)}=\\begin{cases}p_{\\mathrm{exit},t}^{(k)}&\\text{if }k<K_{t}^{\\star},\\\\[2.0pt]\nL151: p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(K_{t}^{\\star})}\\;=\\;\\displaystyle\\sum_{k^{\\prime}=K_{t}^{\\star}}^{K_{\\max}}p_{\\mathrm{exit},t}^{(k^{\\prime})}&\\text{if }k=K_{t}^{\\star},\\end{cases}$$  |  | (7)\nL152: \nL153: which preserves total mass $\\sum_{k=1}^{K_{t}^{\\star}}\\hat{p}_{\\mathrm{exit},t}^{(k)}=1$. Finally, the output embedding is\nL154:  | $$z_{t}^{\\mathrm{final}}=\\sum_{k=1}^{K_{t}^{\\star}}\\hat{p}_{\\mathrm{exit},t}^{(k)}\\,z_{t}^{(k)}.$$  |  | (8)\nL155: \nL156: Importantly, the Router gates $\\{g_{t}^{(k)}\\}$ affect $\\hat{p}_{\\mathrm{exit},t}^{(k)}$ (via the probability flow $p_{\\mathrm{reach}}\\!\\rightarrow\\!p_{\\mathrm{exit}}$), hence gradients from the main CE loss backpropagate to the Router through the mixing weights.\nL157: ### 3.4 Correctness-Aware Adaptive Loss\nL158: \nL159: While probabilistic halting enables token-wise adaptive computation, we additionally need to guide the Router to stop when extra computation is unlikely to help. As evidenced in cite79†Figure 2 , once $p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k)}$ is high, additional latent steps bring little benefit and can even be detrimental. We therefore propose a Correctness-Aware Adaptive Loss that encourages early halting for tokens that are already likely correct, reducing overall FLOPs. Let\nL160:  | $$p_{\\mathrm{target},t}^{(k)}\\triangleq\\bigl[\\mathrm{LMHead}(z_{t}^{(k)})\\bigr]_{y_{t}}$$  |  | (9)\nL161: \nL162: denote the probability assigned to the ground-truth token $y_{t}$ at step $k$. We encourage the model to halt when it is already likely correct by penalizing continuation proportional to this step-wise target probability:\nL163: \nL164:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{adaptive}}=\\lambda\\sum_{t}\\sum_{k=1}^{K_{t}^{\\star}}g_{t}^{(k)}\\cdot\\mathrm{sg}\\!\\left((p_{\\mathrm{target},t}^{(k)})^{\\beta}\\right),$$  |  | (10)\nL165: where $\\mathrm{sg}(\\cdot)$ denotes stop-gradient to prevent degenerate solutions (e.g., reducing $p_{\\mathrm{target}}$ solely to weaken the compute penalty), $\\beta$ controls the nonlinearity of the weighting (larger $\\beta$ concentrates the penalty on high-$p_{\\mathrm{target}}$ steps), and $\\lambda$ scales the overall strength of the adaptive term relative to $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{CE}}$.\nL166: Intuitively, when $p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k)}$ is high, the penalty encourages halting ($g\\!\\to\\!0$); when the model is uncertain, the penalty is small, allowing additional latent computation.\nL167: The overall objective is:\nL168: \nL169:  | $$\\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{CE}}\\!\\Bigl(\\mathrm{LMHead}(z^{\\mathrm{final}}),y\\Bigr)+\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{adaptive}}.$$  |  | (11)\nL170: ### 3.5 Training and Inference Workflows\nL171: \nL172: We now summarize how the above components are integrated in practice.\nL173: cite81†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Inference: token-wise adaptive latent CoT via reach probability. Left: At decoding position $t$, the model iterates latent steps to produce $z_{t}^{(1)},z_{t}^{(2)},\\ldots$ and updates the reach probability, stopping when the next-step reach probability $p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k+1)}$ falls below the threshold $\\tau$. Easy tokens ($x_{2},x_{3}$) stop earlier (shorter latent CoT), while harder tokens ($x_{1}$) continue longer.\nL174: The final representation is obtained by $p_{\\mathrm{exit}}$-weighted mixing of the executed latent states, and is fed to the LM head for prediction. Right: The Router takes each latent hidden state $z_{t}^{(k)}$ and outputs a gate $g_{t}^{(k)}$ (the conditional continuation probability), which defines the probability flow: $p_{\\mathrm{reach}}^{k+1}=p_{\\mathrm{reach}}^{k}g^{k}$ and $p_{\\mathrm{exit}}^{k}=p_{\\mathrm{reach}}^{k}(1-g^{k})$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPretraining with Token-Level Adaptive Latent Chain-of-Thought (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08220v1)\nciteturn31641view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view1\",\"lineno\":220}); Total lines: 343\nL199: ### 4.2 Main Results\nL200: \nL201: ##### Language Modeling Ability.\nL202: \nL203: As shown in cite97†Figure 5 , our method consistently achieves the lowest perplexity across all datasets while using the least training FLOPs. Specifically, our LLaMA-1.4B model with max latent CoT length $\\ell_{\\max}=3$ outperforms the strongest baseline (PonderLM-2) while requiring less than half of the training compute (7.47 vs. 17.47 $\\times 10^{20}$ FLOPs). Moreover, increasing $\\ell_{\\max}$ to 5 further improves performance.\nL204: ##### Downstream Tasks.\nL205: \nL206: Across both zero-shot and five-shot settings and under all compute budgets, our method achieves the best overall average accuracy (cite98†Table 1 ). Notably, our LLaMA-410M with $\\ell_{\\max}=3$ outperforms the compute-comparable vanilla LLaMA-1.4B baseline in average accuracy for both zero-shot and five-shot, indicating that our approach can be even more effective than simply scaling up the parameter count under a matched compute budget.\nL207: Table 1: Zero-shot and five-shot accuracy (%) on downstream tasks. FLOPs denote total pretraining compute (in units of $\\times 10^{20}$), estimated following cite46†Kaplan et al. (2020) . Rows are grouped by compute budget. ^{†} denotes a vanilla baseline with comparable compute.\nL208: Model  | FLOPs  | Lambada (OpenAI)  | Lambada (Std)  | ARC -C  | ARC -E  | Wino Grande  | PIQA  | Hella Swag  | SciQ  | RACE  | Avg. Acc.\nL209: Zero-shot Performance\nL210: Looped LLaMA-410M (4 loops)  | 2.56  | 47.1  | 34.9  | 22.6  | 50.9  | 51.2  | 65.5  | 32.6  | 79.5  | 30.4  | 46.1\nL211: Pause LLaMA-410M (3 pauses)  | 2.56  | 46.3  | 35.3  | 20.9  | 49.9  | 51.7  | 64.7  | 31.7  | 80.1  | 30.4  | 45.7\nL212: PonderLM-410M (3 steps)  | 2.56  | 48.9  | 36.9  | 21.2  | 50.4  | 54.1  | 66.3  | 33.3  | 80.6  | 30.4  | 46.9\nL213: PonderLM2-410M (1 step)  | 5.12  | 48.7  | 37.7  | 21.0  | 50.6  | 51.3  | 65.1  | 33.0  | 79.8  | 30.8  | 46.4\nL214: MoR-410M (4 Recursions)  | 2.27  | 43.1  | 32.0  | 19.8  | 49.5  | 51.7  | 64.9  | 31.1  | 80.8  | 30.9  | 44.9\nL215: LLaMA-1.4B (train from scratch)^{†}  | 2.18  | 50.6  | 37.1  | 20.9  | 52.2  | 53.0  | 65.6  | 33.6  | 84.5  | 31.8  | 47.7\nL216: Our LLaMA-410M ($\\ell_{\\max}=3$)  | 2.27  | 49.3  | 40.2  | 23.6  | 50.8  | 53.7  | 65.9  | 33.9  | 82.2  | 33.0  | 48.1\nL217: Looped LLaMA-1.4B (4 loops)  | 8.74  | 55.8  | 45.6  | 23.2  | 55.2  | 54.1  | 68.9  | 37.6  | 84.9  | 33.0  | 50.9\nL218: Pause LLaMA-1.4B (3 pauses)  | 8.74  | 56.2  | 46.5  | 24.2  | 54.0  | 55.3  | 68.8  | 36.7  | 85.4  | 32.3  | 51.0\nL219: PonderLM-1.4B (3 steps)  | 8.74  | 56.7  